3. STORE BOOKS IN STATE
================================================================================

Create the books state:

const [books, setBooks] = useState([]);


Explanation:

- books contains the books returned by the API.
- setBooks() updates the books state.
- [] means the application initially contains no books.


After API response:

const data = await response.json();

const bookList = data.items || [];

setBooks(bookList);


Flow:

API
 |
 v
data.items
 |
 v
setBooks()
 |
 v
books State Updated
 |
 v
React Re-renders


================================================================================
4. USE map() TO DISPLAY BOOKS
================================================================================

map() is commonly used in React to convert an array into JSX elements.

Example:

{books.map((book) => (

  <div key={book.id}>

    <h3>
      {book.volumeInfo?.title}
    </h3>

  </div>

))}


Explanation:

- books is the array containing book objects.
- map() loops through every book.
- book represents the current book.
- JSX is returned for every book.
- React displays the generated JSX elements.
- key={book.id} gives React a stable identifier.


================================================================================
5. HOW map() WORKS
================================================================================

Suppose books contains:

books = [
  { id: '1', title: 'Java' },
  { id: '2', title: 'React' },
  { id: '3', title: 'Spring Boot' }
];


map() processes:

Iteration 1
-> Java

Iteration 2
-> React

Iteration 3
-> Spring Boot


Conceptually:

books.map(book => createCard(book))


Result:

Book 1 -> Card 1

Book 2 -> Card 2

Book 3 -> Card 3


================================================================================
6. WHY DOES REACT NEED A key?
================================================================================

Each element generated from map() should have a unique key.

Recommended:

key={book.id}


Example:

{books.map((book) => (

  <div key={book.id}>
    {book.volumeInfo?.title}
  </div>

))}


Why?

- Helps React identify individual list items.
- Helps React efficiently update the UI.
- Prevents unnecessary list-rendering problems.


Prefer:

key={book.id}


Instead of:

key={index}


Reason:

- book.id represents the actual book.
- Array positions can change when data changes.
- A stable API ID is usually a better React key.


================================================================================
7. SIMPLE BOOK DISPLAY
================================================================================

Example:

<div className="row">

  {books.map((book) => (

    <div
      key={book.id}
      className="col-md-4 mb-4"
    >

      <div className="card">

        <div className="card-body">

          <h5 className="card-title">

            {book.volumeInfo?.title || 'No Title'}

          </h5>


          <p className="card-text">

            {book.volumeInfo?.authors?.join(', ')
              || 'Unknown Author'}

          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  ))}

</div>


WHAT THE CODE DOES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- books.map() loops through the books array.

- key={book.id} uniquely identifies each card.

- col-md-4 creates a Bootstrap grid column.

- mb-4 adds spacing below each card.

- card creates the Bootstrap card.

- card-body contains the actual book information.

- book.volumeInfo?.title gets the book title.

- || 'No Title' provides a fallback when title is unavailable.

- authors is an array.

- join(', ') converts the authors array into readable text.


Example:

["John Smith", "David Miller"]

becomes:

John Smith, David Miller


================================================================================
8. HANDLING MISSING API DATA
================================================================================

Not every book returned by an API contains every field.

One book may have:

- Title
- Authors
- Description
- Image

Another may only contain:

- Title


Therefore this can be dangerous:

book.volumeInfo.authors.join(', ')


If authors is missing, the application can fail.


Use optional chaining:

book.volumeInfo?.authors?.join(', ')


Add a fallback:

book.volumeInfo?.authors?.join(', ')
  || 'Unknown Author'


================================================================================
9. OPTIONAL CHAINING
================================================================================

Optional chaining uses:

?.


Example:

book.volumeInfo?.title


Meaning:

"If volumeInfo exists, access title."


Comparison:

---------------------------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
Code                                         | Missing Data              | Result
---------------------------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
book.volumeInfo.title                        | volumeInfo missing        | Runtime error
book.volumeInfo?.title                       | volumeInfo missing        | undefined
book.volumeInfo?.title || 'No Title'         | title missing             | No Title
book.volumeInfo?.authors?.join(', ')         | authors missing           | undefined
book.volumeInfo?.authors?.join(', ')         | authors exists            | Joined authors
---------------------------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------


Recommended:

{book.volumeInfo?.title || 'No Title'}

{book.volumeInfo?.authors?.join(', ')
  || 'Unknown Author'}


================================================================================
10. DISPLAY THE BOOK COVER
================================================================================

The thumbnail can normally be accessed from:

book.volumeInfo.imageLinks.thumbnail


Because imageLinks may be missing, check it first:

{book.volumeInfo?.imageLinks?.thumbnail && (

  <img
    src={book.volumeInfo.imageLinks.thumbnail}
    className="card-img-top"
    alt={book.volumeInfo?.title || 'Book cover'}
  />

)}


Explanation:

- imageLinks?.thumbnail safely checks for an image.
- && means render the image only when a thumbnail exists.
- src contains the book image URL.
- alt provides accessible alternative text.
- card-img-top applies Bootstrap image styling.


Flow:

Thumbnail Exists?
      |
   +--+--+
   |     |
  Yes    No
   |     |
   v     v
Show    Don't
Image   Render Image


